  input — created on first need, reused after, emptied when the field passes. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m has no stylesheet at all today,
  so this needs a small [38;5;153m<style>[39m block ([38;5;153m.field-error { color: #b00020; display: block; font-size: .875rem }[39m) or the
  messages render as invisible black text in the flow.

  One deliberate detail: [38;5;153mrequired()[39m trims before testing, so a space-bar-only field is empty — but the value handed to
  [38;5;153monValid[39m is never mutated.

  [1mDesign — Section 4: Testing, migration, and what's out[0m

  [1mTests[0m ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m, no dependencies) cover the core: each rule's pass and fail cases, first-failure-wins ordering, the
  empty-skips-non-required invariant, multi-field schemas, extra values with no schema entry, and the empty schema.

  [1mThe honest gap:[0m [38;5;153mbind-form.js[39m gets no automated test. Testing it needs a DOM, which means jsdom (breaks zero-dep) or
  Playwright (you declined it). It'll be verified by hand in the browser. That's roughly half the new code running on
  manual verification — the cheapest fix later is jsdom as a single devDependency, if the gap starts to bite.

  [1mMigration.[0m [38;5;153mapp.js[39m loses [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m and the inline submit listener, and gains a schema plus one [38;5;153mbindForm[39m call;
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m and [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m are unchanged. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153mname[39m attributes on the two inputs, [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m on the script
  tag, and the style block. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are not touched. One line goes in the README about serving
  over HTTP, since [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m breaks opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly and nothing else would explain it.

  [1mDeliberately out[0m (say so now, cheap to add later): cross-field rules, async/server rules, revalidation on blur or
  input, message i18n, and pluggable renderers.

  [1mOne open question before I write this up.[0m The whole point is reuse, but the repo has exactly one form — so "reusable"
  would be proven by the API's shape and the core's tests, not by an actual second consumer. Reuse claims with one
  consumer are how APIs end up accidentally shaped around that single case.

